ul_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"q