e-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164571" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,y,\\theta )$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-cap