theta "] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-164576" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/state.json" "file_sha256" "3710e4c5d31ce4e3f9a675ffa398abacc80a1ab3c7038e133629e862488dd7a8" "basis" 2812 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-worke